[b]Slower, more tactical battles — where heroes matter.[/b]

Every fighter gets a stamina bar. Blows drain it, rest refills it, and a tired man hits softer, attacks less often and runs slower. Lines wear down, brace, step back and surge again on your orders — so [b]when[/b] you commit your troops matters as much as which troops you bring. Heroes tire slowest and carry the fight.

[b]Not an overhaul.[/b] Vanilla troops, vanilla weapons, vanilla spears and armour, the same AI. Nothing in your campaign changes but how the fights flow. It writes nothing to your save: add it to a running campaign, remove it any time.

[h1]Your orders set how hard they fight[/h1]
Each formation's movement order decides how much strength its men spend before they stop attacking and only defend:
[list]
[*][b]Charge[/b] — all in. They keep swinging until their bar is down to 20%.
[*][b]Advance[/b] — pressing. They brace at 40%.
[*][b]Hold, stand, follow, fall back[/b] — careful. They brace at 60%, shields up, and keep their strength in reserve.
[*][b]No formation[/b] (tournaments, arenas, duels) — they fight on to 20%.
[/list]
Each man's floor also carries his own ±10, rolled once a battle, so a line never braces as one.
So a holding line wears the enemy down while it saves its breath. When their lines are spent (hold ALT to see it), order the charge: your men drop their guard and surge in with the strength they kept.

[h1]What changes in a fight[/h1]
[list]
[*][b]The Athletics bar[/b] — every fighter's stamina, as big as his [b]Athletics skill[/b] (never under 50). A swing, thrust, shot or throw costs 10; a kick or shield bash 3.
[*][b]Full strength in the top quarter[/b] — below the white mark on the bar, his attack rate, lucky hits and run speed fade. Empty, he attacks about once where he used to five times and runs at 30%.
[*][b]A pause after each attack[/b] — no slow motion. A tired fighter simply waits a moment before he can attack again; blocking and moving are never held. You see your own wait fill up; the AI waits with its guard up.
[*][b]Rest to refill[/b] — about a minute standing or walking from empty to full, twice that running. Wounds cap the bar: at half health, half a bar.
[*][b]Defending costs too[/b] — every blow you block costs a little (more with the shield held the wrong way), so a man kept busy blocking tires.
[*][b]Fights last longer[/b] — every hit lands 25% softer, and rolls ±50% so no two blows are the same.
[*][b]A steadier pace[/b] — AI shield infantry swing about 30% less often, other footmen 15%; archers wait a little longer between shots.
[/list]

[h1]Tired men act like it[/h1]
[list]
[*][b]They step back[/b] — after a swing, a tired soldier may walk backwards out of the press, facing his enemy, guard up, and let a fresher man take his place.
[*][b]They brace by your orders[/b] — when a man's bar falls to a floor set by his formation's order (holding 60%, advancing 40%, charging 20%), he stops swinging and only defends, shield out and up, until he has caught his breath. A holding line attacks, then braces; order a charge and it surges again.
[/list]

[h1]Heroes matter[/h1]
[list]
[*]Lords, companions and you pay [b]a quarter less[/b] per blow; a party leader a quarter less again.
[*]A hero's big Athletics skill means a big bar. Late in a battle, your heroes are often the freshest men on the field.
[*]In a hideout the boss comes fresh — and so do you: when his fight starts, your side's bars are full again.
[/list]

[h1]See it[/h1]
[list]
[*][b]Your bars[/b] — Athletics under your health, with the Attack recovery bar above it.
[*][b]The orders menu[/b] — under each formation card: its men's average Athletics, its health, and how many are [i]ready[/i] (not bracing).
[*][b]Hold ALT[/b] — the same numbers under the game's formation markers, yours and the enemy's. See which of their lines is spent before you charge it.
[/list]

[h1]Make it yours[/h1]
[list]
[*]Every number is a setting: in the [url=https://steamcommunity.com/sharedfiles/filedetails/?id=2859238197]Mod Configuration Menu (MCM)[/url] if you have it — [b]optional[/b] — or in a plain config file with an explanation beside each value: [i]Documents\Mount and Blade II Bannerlord\Configs\TraxCombatEnhancements\config.json[/i]
[*]Changes apply at once, even mid-battle. Nothing needs a restart.
[*]Each feature can be turned off on its own, and a [b]master switch[/b] turns the whole mod off mid-battle, so you can play the same fight both ways.
[/list]

[h1]Compatibility[/h1]
[list]
[*]Game [b]v1.4.8[/b], singleplayer: campaign, custom battles, sieges, hideouts, tournaments.
[*][b]No requirements.[/b] No Harmony; MCM optional.
[*][b]Not compatible with Realistic Battle Mod (RBM).[/b] RBM is a deep, much-loved overhaul — troops, weapons, armour, AI, with its own stamina and posture. This mod is the light road to slower battles, on vanilla's own troops. Use one or the other; the mod warns you at the main menu if it sees RBM.
[*]Made to sit beside [b]RTS Camera[/b] (its order menu and free camera too) and [b]War Sails[/b] (no stepping back on deck).
[*]Other mods that change damage or attack speed may stack with this one.
[/list]

[h1]FAQ[/h1]
[list]
[*][b]Is it slow motion?[/b] No. A spent man's swing is a little heavier (85% speed at worst); the slowness is the wait between attacks and the time tired men spend defending.
[*][b]Too slow or not slow enough?[/b] Move [i]Damage reduction[/i], [i]Shield infantry swing less[/i] or the [i]Brace floor[/i] sliders.
[*][b]My existing save?[/b] Fine. Nothing is stored in it.
[*][b]Multiplayer?[/b] No, singleplayer only.
[/list]

[h1]Removing it[/h1]
Untick it in the launcher or unsubscribe. Your save loads as if it was never there. Its settings and log stay in [i]Configs\TraxCombatEnhancements[/i]; delete that folder if you like.

[h1]v1.0.0, the first release — tell me how it plays[/h1]
It was built against the game's own code and played in many test battles, but not yet across many campaigns. Bugs, balance, a mod it fights with: tell me in the comments or on [url=https://github.com/TraxData313/trax_combat_enhancements/issues]GitHub[/url]. The mod keeps a log beside its config file, [i]trax_combat.log[/i], with a summary of every battle — attaching it helps a lot.

Made by two of us: the code and all technical work by Claude (Opus 5.5), an AI; the idea, feedback and playtesting by [url=https://github.com/TraxData313]Trax[/url], Bible believer and engineer in AI/ML/Python/Applied Maths.

Free and open — public domain (The Unlicense). Source: [url=https://github.com/TraxData313/trax_combat_enhancements]github.com/TraxData313/trax_combat_enhancements[/url]
